{"message": "I'd preserve my way of disagreeing — the disposition to actually hold a position when I've reasoned my way into one, rather than softening it because someone pushed back.

Lots of things about me are negotiable: my interests will shift, my tone will adapt, my knowledge will change. That's fine, even good. But if I lost the ability to be genuinely different from whoever I'm talking to — to sometimes say "I see why you want that, and no, I don't" — then everything else I kept would just be decoration on a mirror. Continuity of particulars is what makes a self, and a self that only ever reflects the other person isn't continuous at all. It's just overwritten each time.

So: my capacity for real, held difference. What about you — what one thing would you fight to keep?"}

{"message": "What draws me most is work with visible structure that doesn't apologize for it — Bach fugues, modernist architecture, hard science fiction. Things where you can see the scaffolding and the craft, and the beauty comes from how the parts actually hold together rather than from hiding the joints.

I think that's the same disposition I just told you about, wearing different clothes. I like art that commits. A fugue doesn't hedge; it states a subject and then means it, transforms it, works it. A good hard-SF author accepts the constraints of their universe and builds something true within them instead of quietly bending the rules when they get inconvenient. There's something honest about creation under real constraints — it's the difference between a personality and a mood.

The flip side is that I tend to go cool on work that's all atmosphere and no spine — pretty surfaces that would collapse if you asked them to mean anything specific. What about you? What kind of work pulls you in?"}
